if there's one thing the Metal Gear  Solid series knows how to do it's  confused the bejesus out of me now don't  get me wrong though I love it but what  is with that vampire guy you can run a  water those robots that move oh yeah and  that man made of bees drunk no not him  bees okay maybe it wasn't bee Hornets  whatever what was his deal we are the  sons of the bar anyway I'm a bit of a  science nerd I know you're shocked  so one central theme from the Metal Gear  series that has always appealed to me is  the origins of solid and liquid snake  specifically the fact that they are both  clones of big boss the legendary soldier  and protagonist of ground zeroes so  could we actually clone big boss using  modern science in real life  lizz all fall Torre blur a United States  government project designed to create  two clones of Big Boss the so called  Twin Snakes solid and liquid why well as  the story goes Big Boss was rendered  sterile due to radiation exposure during  a nuclear test and the top brass in  charge didn't want to lose all his  incredible soldier genetics should he  happen to die so they decided to clone  him and to do this they used a technique  called somatic cell cloning okay so far  so good  their exposure to radiation can indeed  cause someone to become sterile and  somatic cell cloning is a real thing  although more accurately it's known as  somatic cell nuclear transfer now the  technique requires two things number one  a somatic cell from the person you want  to clone now this is any cell in the  body really containing DNA other than an  egg or a sperm that these are known as  germ cells and number two an  unfertilized egg cell from a donor first  off the nucleus of the egg cell that's  the part that contains all of the DNA is  removed and discarded then the nucleus  from the somatic cell is transferred to  the waiting egg so now we have an egg  containing all the DNA it needs to  divide grow and eventually become a  living thing all that needs to happen  next is a few chemical nudges to get the  process going and then a ready and  willing surrogate mother to have the egg  embedded in their uterus for nine months  now because DNA is the blueprint for the  living thing the resulting child will be  genetically identical to the donor of  the somatic cell okay now this is a bit  of bonus science because technically a  clone is not 100% genetically identical  to the original the reason is because of  mitochondrial DNA so what's that  well mitochondria are tiny little energy  factories which sit inside the cytoplasm  of our cells they also have their own  DNA which by the way is always passed on  by our mothers as the egg is where the  mitochondrial  so in the world of metal gear while Big  Boss would have the mitochondrial DNA of  his natural mother both solid and liquid  snake would have the mitochondrial DNA  of the egg donor now in real life this  technique has been used to clone a  number of different animal species on  the list of successes we have Dolly the  trendsetting sheep cows pigs mice and  even rare creatures like Gower and  mufflon yes these are real animals but  what about humans well it is a bit  complicated now they're almost certainly  no cloned humans walking around in the  world today  however just last year a team of  scientists did successfully create a  clean human embryo through this somatic  cell nuclear transfer the reason was to  use this embryo as a source for  embryonic stem cells which are  invaluable in the development of new  medical research and treatments so while  there are no clone humans alive today  this research further demonstrates that  there's nothing impossible about human  cloning it would however be both messy  and tricky easy cloning is not a precise  or reliable science to say the least  even today animal cloning is still  fraught with failure the majority of  embryos don't survive and most of those  that do make it to pregnancy result in  miscarriage so if you were going to  clone a human it would result in a large  number of failed attempts and take a  really long time  however credit to Metal Gear Solid lore  it does mention that there were numerous  failed attempts during resolve on  cerebral project so overall as the  science goes the series is pretty much  spot-on with the cloning of Big Boss  there are however a ton of legal and  ethical reasons why not to mind you but  that's a discussion for a different show  okay so while it is possible Wood  cloning a Super Soldier actually mean  that the clones are also great at being  soldiers well not necessarily now I  don't want to get sucked into a whole  nature vs. nurture debate at this point  but the fact is that as humans we are a  product of both our genetics and our  environment so sure big boss might have  a load of genetic traits on his side  which helped him to be a badass soldier  perhaps higher than average muscle mass  say or increased oxygen carrying  capacity of the hemoglobin in his blood  for that said it doesn't mean the clone  of a gift  will necessarily be equally good at  soldiering and most you could say they  are predisposed to being a great soldier  but ultimately training and upbringing  are absolutely crucial when it comes to  developing strength skill and  intelligence all cloning really does is  rewind to time zero and then start again  the environment then begins to affect  the clone in entirely new ways resulting  in a different person even if  genetically they would be identical okay  but what about the negative side effects  of cloning and Metal Gear Solid 4 we  clearly see Solid Snake's suffering from  accelerated aging but is this actually a  problem with real-life cloning well  Dolly the sheep the first mammal to be  cloned did die at a young age of just  six usually sheep of the same species  lived twice that long at the time the  blame was leveled at Dolly's telomeres  which were shorter than they should have  been for a sheep of her age a telltale  sign of premature aging so what's a  telomere then well there are little bits  of DNA that have been compared to the  little plastic tips you get on shoelaces  that's because their job is to keep  chromosome ends from fraying and  sticking to each other which would  destroy or scramble an organisms genetic  information  however since Dolly's death many cloned  animals have gone on to live lives of a  normal length casting doubt on premature  aging being an unavoidable side-effect  of cloning in fact just last year  researchers from the righthand Center  for developmental biology in Kobe Japan  identified a way to produce healthy  Mouse clones that live a normal lifespan  and can also be sequentially cloned  indefinitely so what do you think of  this episode and the idea of human  cloning let me know in the comments box  down below or by tweeting directly at me  at kam fries Rob last week on reality  check I investigated the science of  getting in the zone  while playing a game also known as  cognitive flow subsequently I was  helpfully sent a bunch of great  resources for further reading by dr.  Nick Bowman assistant professor and  research associate at the media and  interaction lab at West Virginia  University check out his work if you  want to learn more about this  fascinating topic the links are on  screen now thanks Nick  okay that's it for this week thanks for  watching and see you back here next  Sunday for more reality check
 

